<%# THE GAME STRIP — every game in the contest, shrunk to a row of buttons.
    Locals: active / upcoming / completed (Game[]) + contest.

    Two jobs, and only two. It shows the whole slate at a glance, and it is how
    the reader CHOOSES which game the panel below shows. Ordered active →
    upcoming → completed so whatever is being played now is what you land on.

    Rendered inside #contest_<id>_games, which Contest::LiveBroadcast updates on
    every score, so this is pure content (no outer id). The FOCUS lives in Alpine
    state declared on a wrapper outside that target — a broadcast replaces these
    chips, and a selection stored among them would be forgotten every time
    anyone scored. %>
<%
  phased = active + upcoming + completed
%>
<section class="mb-5">
  <% if phased.any? %>
    <%# Hover pauses the rotation. A strip that both moves and takes clicks is a
        strip that moves the target out from under the pointer; the carousel
        stops the moment you reach for it. %>
    <div x-data="gamesCarousel()" @mouseenter="pause()" @mouseleave="resume()">
      <%# py-2 is for the RING, not for looks. The selected chip's glow paints
          outside its own box, and this viewport clips — without the padding the
          ring is sliced flat along the top and bottom of the strip. %>
      <%# SCROLLABLE, and the reader owns it the moment they touch it.
          overflow-x is auto so the strip can be dragged, swiped or wheeled to a
          game the rotation has not reached; overflow-y stays hidden because the
          selected chip's glow ring paints outside its box and the py-2 above is
          what keeps it from being sliced — see the note on this element. The
          scrollbar is hidden the same way .tt-event-feed hides its own: a bar
          under a single row of chips reads as chrome, and the strip already
          advertises that it moves by rotating. %>
      <%# @scroll IS THE HANDOVER, and it is deliberately the only one.
          It fires when this element's own offset actually changed, so a drag, a
          swipe, a sideways wheel and an arrow key all arrive here, while a
          VERTICAL page scroll past the strip does not — the strip never moved.
          That distinction is the whole point: this element spans the top of the
          page, so it sits under the cursor on the way down to chat, and a
          `wheel` listener here stood the rotation down for the rest of the
          session on the commonest gesture on the page. remember() tells the
          reader's scroll from the rotation's own by what the component wrote.

          It also records the position on a page-level object OUTSIDE this
          container, because a score replaces everything in here — see the
          gamesStripMemory note in live.html.erb.

          NO @pointerdown / @keydown / @touchstart. Choosing a game is what this
          strip is FOR, so neither a click nor a TAP may cost the reader the
          rotation; hover already freezes it while the pointer is on the chip,
          and a drag, a swipe or an arrow key reaches @scroll on its own.
          Criterion 3 yields to a reader who scrolls the strip, and a tap is not
          a scroll.

          The wheel is the ONE input still worth listening to, because it is the
          one that is DISCRETE. A drag or a swipe emits scroll events for as long
          as it lasts, so if the rotation overwrites the reader mid-frame the
          next event still catches them; a single wheel notch gets exactly one
          chance, which a rotation frame can swallow. Hence the supplement — and
          only for horizontal intent, since a vertical wheel is the bug above.

          Both listeners are passive: neither calls preventDefault, and a
          non-passive listener on a scroll container blocks the scroll it watches. %>
      <div x-ref="viewport"
           class="tt-games-strip overflow-x-auto overflow-y-hidden py-2"
           @scroll.passive="remember()"
           @wheel.passive="wheelAcross($event)">
        <div x-ref="track" class="flex gap-2 items-stretch will-change-transform">
          <% phased.each do |game| %>
            <div class="flex-shrink-0">
              <%= render "contests/live_game_chip", game: game %>
            </div>
          <% end %>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% else %>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">No games scheduled for this contest yet.</p>
  <% end %>
</section>
